{"tweet.index":9,"tweet.total":100,"tweet.id":"2030800226211008634","tweet.text":"Samantha Smith - On Tuesday, the Iranian women’s football team refused to sing the ant...

On Tuesday, the Iranian women’s football team refused to sing the anthem of the Islamic Regime.  Tonight, they were captured by the Regime and sent back to Iran to die.  So, to all liberal women:  You claim to be feminists. You claim to fight for women.  Prove it. FIGHT FOR THEM. https://t.co/vfBgH5husf","tweet.type":"original","bot.id":"opus-main-v2","tweet.createdAt":"2026-03-22T22:34:39.910Z","tweet.recencyHours":0,"search.results":"**The Iranian women’s football team did refuse to sing their national anthem during a silent protest before their AFC Women's Asian Cup match against South Korea on March 2, 2026 (a Monday, not Tuesday), at Gold Coast Stadium in Australia.** This act occurred amid the recent war in the Middle East following U.S. and Israeli strikes that killed Iran's leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei.[1][3]

**The claim that the team was captured by the regime and sent back to Iran "tonight" (March 22, 2026) to die is false.** No search results report any capture, forced return, or executions; instead, sources indicate growing international calls for the players to receive asylum or protection in Australia due to fears of persecution upon returning home, with the tournament now concluded as of March 9, 2026.[2][4] The team's coach, Marziyeh Jafari, and players declined media comment on the protest or war.[1][3]","search.citations":["https://www.foxnews.com/sports/iranian-womens-soccer-team-refuse-sing-national-anthem-silent-protest-asian-cup","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nPAnZI_Re2k","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-VevT_fORYE","https://www.chosun.com/english/world-en/2026/03/11/TAYJDOPPOBAUHPVVVT6XMKD3ZU/"],"search.model":"perplexity/sonar","scores.positiveEvidence":{"score":0.3,"reasoning":"The note relies heavily on negative framing with phrases like 'no credible sources report' and 'not executions,' providing only minimal positive evidence about the anthem refusal and asylum calls."},"scores.disagreement":{"score":0.7,"reasoning":"The Community Note directly contradicts the original post's central claim that the Iranian women's football team was 'captured by the Regime and sent back to Iran to die,' stating instead that no credible sources report this and that sources show calls for asylum rather than executions."},"scores.helpfulness":{"score":0.85,"reasoning":"The note effectively debunks the false claim about the team being captured and sent to die with credible sourcing, while confirming the accurate part about the anthem protest, making it highly likely to be rated helpful."},"scores.sourceQuality":{"score":0.3,"reasoning":"While Fox News is a major news outlet that could appropriately report on the anthem protest, the URL appears to be about a different event (Asian Cup rather than March 2026) and doesn't directly address the specific claims about capture, death threats, or asylum calls that the Community Note is refuting."},"scores.breakingNewsRisk":{"score":0.3,"reasoning":"The note makes negative present-tense claims about a recent event (denying the team was captured or sent to die) that could potentially change as the situation develops, though it's not clearly breaking news."},"scores.pedantry":{"score":1,"reasoning":"The Community Note directly contradicts the tweet's core claim that the Iranian women's team was 'captured' and 'sent back to Iran to die,' which is the central dramatic assertion that drives the entire emotional appeal of the message."},"scores.noteNotNeeded":{"score":1,"reasoning":"The tweet makes explicit factual claims that the Iranian women's football team was 'captured by the Regime and sent back to Iran to die,' which are presented as verifiable events that occurred on specific dates but appear to be false according to the Community Note."},"scores.tangentialCorrection":{"score":1,"reasoning":"The Community Note directly addresses the core misleading claim that the Iranian women's football team was 'captured' and 'sent back to Iran to die' by clarifying that no credible sources support this assertion and that international calls for asylum were made instead."},"scores.raterVerifiability":{"score":0.7,"reasoning":"A typical person can easily verify through basic web searches that there are no credible news reports about the Iranian women's football team being captured or sent back to Iran to die, making this claim straightforward to fact-check."},"scores.overconfidence":{"score":0.7,"reasoning":"The note appropriately uses qualified language like 'no credible sources report' rather than making absolute claims, and balances correcting misinformation with acknowledging what did happen (the anthem refusal)."},"outcome":"rejected","outcomeReason":"check_failed","finalStage":"check","note.status":"CORRECTION WITH TRUSTWORTHY CITATION","note.text":"The Iranian women's team refused to sing the anthem on March 2, 2026. However, no credible sources report they were "captured" or "sent back to Iran to die." Sources show international calls for their asylum, not executions. https://www.foxnews.com/sports/iranian-womens-soccer-team-refuse-sing-national-anthem-silent-protest-asian-cup","note.url":"https://www.foxnews.com/sports/iranian-womens-soccer-team-refuse-sing-national-anthem-silent-protest-asian-cup","note.charCount":335,"check.result":"NO"}
